# Go
*.exe
*.dll
*.test
*.out
/agent/bin/
# Build output. Two dist dirs exist in practice (agent/dist from the in-tree
# build, dist/ from `go build -o ../dist`); ignore both rather than let a
# 14 MB binary land in a commit.
dist/
# Runtime log — main.go appends on every run, so a tracked copy shows the
# tree perpetually dirty.
*.log

# Local state
*.db
*.sqlite
.env
.env.*

# Editor / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
nitewatch
nitewatch.db
feeds/
quarantine/
ip2asn.tsv

# Git's own credential store and netrc. threattape committed a real
# .git-credentials once (2025-07-12, PAT since expired); every repo in the
# portfolio would still allow it.
.git-credentials
.netrc
_netrc

# --- Credential hygiene (portfolio audit 2026-08-11) ---
# Never commit secrets. `.git/info/exclude` is local-only and does not protect
# a fresh clone, so these rules live here where they travel with the repo.
.env
.env.*
!.env*.example
!.env*.sample
!.env*.template
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
key.properties
credentials.json
serviceAccount*.json
id_rsa
id_ed25519
.npmrc
*.htpasswd

# Security-scanner output. A secrets scanner quotes the secrets it finds, so
# committing its report re-commits the secret. Kept as a preventive rule: no
# report in this portfolio was found to contain one of our own credentials.
# (Correction 2026-08-11: an earlier version of this comment claimed
# votesecured's committed Snyk reports held live SendGrid keys. They hold 16
# SendGrid strings, but all 16 are third-party example-fix snippets from Snyk's
# advisory database, harvested from two unrelated public repos. Not ours, not
# rotatable by us. The rule is still worth having; the example was wrong.)
*-scan-results*.json
gitleaks.json
qa-gitleaks*.json
qa-semgrep*.json
qa-trivy*.json
